.TH LOSTC 1 "2026-08-01" "lost-commander 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lostc \- terminal file manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lostc
.RI [ LEFT_DIR ]
.RI [ RIGHT_DIR ]
.br
.B lostc
.B \-\-list
.RI [ DIR ]
.br
.B lostc
.RB [ \-\-help | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lostc
is a file manager in the Norton Commander tradition: a directory panel,
function keys along the bottom, and a command line under it. It opens with
one panel, which is XTree's arrangement rather than Norton's, and a second is
a keystroke away when something needs two directories at once. Everything it does to the filesystem is shared with
.BR lostc\-gui (1),
which is the same program drawn in a window.
.PP
With no arguments both panels open on the current directory. One argument
sets the left panel, two set both - the second is used when the second panel
is opened.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-list " " [\fIDIR\fR]
Print what a panel would show for \fIDIR\fR and exit. The whole listing
pipeline with no terminal attached, which makes it useful for scripting and
for checking that a build works.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a summary of the keys and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH COMMAND LINE PLACEHOLDERS
.B %f
is the file under the cursor,
.B %s
the marked names (or the cursor file when nothing is marked),
.B %d
the other panel's directory, and
.B %%
a literal percent. Each name is quoted for the shell, and the status row
shows the expanded line before Enter runs it.
.SH UNDO
.B Alt\-Z
reads the last file operation out of the account and shows exactly what
reversing it would do before anything moves; Enter goes ahead. A copy
changed since is left alone, a move whose original seat is taken is left
alone, and each is said with its reason.
.B Alt\-H
shows what was done to the things in the current folder, in the viewer.
.SH QUICK FILTER
.B Alt\-F
narrows the listing to names containing what you type; Enter keeps the
filter, Escape takes it off, and changing directory takes it off too. The
function keys act on what the filter leaves visible, and marks made before
the filter went on survive being hidden by it.
.SH PINNED COMMANDS
.B pin <command>
on the command line keeps that line on top of the current folder's history,
until the same
.B pin
takes it off again. The line is kept as typed, so a template with
.B %f
in it stays a template.
.SH HISTORY SEARCH
.B Alt\-R
starts a reverse search over everything that has been run, offering matches
from the current directory first; typing narrows it,
.B Alt\-R
again steps to the next match, Enter takes the offered line onto the command
line without running it, and Escape gives back exactly what was typed before.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Tab
The other panel, opening it when only one is showing.
.TP
.B F12
Show or hide the second panel.
.TP
.B Enter
Open: descend into a directory, walk into an archive, or hand a file to the
desktop.
.TP
.B Backspace
The parent directory, when nothing is typed on the command line.
.TP
.BR F1 - F10
Help, rename, view, edit, copy, move, mkdir, delete, sort, quit. Letters go
to the command line, so
.B q
does not quit.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Q
Quit. There is a second way out because a terminal may keep
.B F10
for its own menu and never pass it on. GTK binds it to the menubar for every
application, so GNOME Terminal, Terminator and Tilix take it by default: the
keystroke is consumed above the pseudo\-terminal and nothing running inside
can see it. Worse, the menubar then has the keyboard, and a
.B q
there is File \(-> Quit \- which closes the terminal and every tab in it.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-C
Cancel a running copy or move; with nothing running, clear the command line;
with neither, quit \- which is what the keystroke means everywhere else in a
terminal.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-Z
Suspend, giving the terminal back to the shell;
.B fg
resumes. Windows has no job control, so there it does nothing.
.TP
.B Space
Mark the file under the cursor, when nothing is typed.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-H
Show or hide hidden files.
.TP
.B Alt\-T
The directory tree, above the files. \fBEnter\fR opens a directory and drops
into its files; \fBEscape\fR climbs back to the tree.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-J
The journal: an account of what has been done.
.SH COMMAND LINE
Typing goes to the command line under the panels, and \fBEnter\fR runs it in
the directory the panel is showing. The program hands the terminal over while
it runs, so interactive commands, colour and pagers all work, and waits for a
key before taking the screen back.
.PP
An empty command line means the panels: \fBSpace\fR marks, \fBBackspace\fR
climbs, \fBEscape\fR returns to the tree. Once something is typed they are
characters.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/lost\-commander/settings.toml
Settings: theme, chosen shell, window arrangement.
.TP
.I ~/.config/lost\-commander/bookmarks.toml
Saved local and network locations.
.TP
.I ~/.config/lost\-commander/journal/
The account of what was done, one file a day.
.PP
On macOS these are under
.IR ~/Library/Application\ Support/lost\-commander/ ,
and on Windows under
.IR %APPDATA%\\lost\-commander\\ .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B EDITOR
.TQ
.B VISUAL
What \fBF4\fR opens a file with. \fBVISUAL\fR wins.
.TP
.B SHELL
The shell used for the command line, on Unix.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on a clean quit, non-zero if the terminal could not be set up.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lostc\-gui (1),
.BR mc (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Alexander Garmash.
.SH BUGS
Report at https://github.com/GarAlex/lost-commander/issues
